## 7. Structured Metadata and Content Model

Implement a strictly typed content model.

The following metadata categories are required or optional according to the content type.

### Identity

- Stable content ID.
- Slug.
- Title.
- Optional subtitle.
- Description or abstract.
- Language.
- Optional translation group ID.

### Classification

- Content type.
- Authority classification.
- Editorial status.
- Publication status.
- Official approval status, when applicable.
- Knowledge depth.

### Organization

- Project journey stages.
- Official project phase references.
- Module references.
- Platform domain references.
- Topics.
- Tags.

### Traceability

- Official document ID, when available.
- Decision IDs.
- Requirement IDs.
- Research IDs.
- Future consideration IDs.
- Independent review IDs.
- Related content IDs.

### Source Provenance

- Source repository or publisher.
- Original source path or URL.
- Source title.
- Source revision or commit SHA, when verified.
- Source tree SHA, where appropriate.
- Source document date, when available.
- Review date, when available.
- Publication date, when available.
- Source language.
- Attribution.
- Original copyright or intellectual property notice.
- Publication authorization metadata.

### Versioning

- Content revision.
- Optional source version.
- Optional previous revision reference.
- Optional supersedes reference.
- Optional last verified date.

Do not invent dates or use the current application build date as an official source date.

Unknown metadata must remain null, omitted, or explicitly unknown.

Use discriminated unions or similarly appropriate TypeScript structures where they improve correctness.

Avoid one oversized model containing hundreds of unrelated optional properties.

